----This is a sequence problemm, not a counting problemm
-- Write your PostgreSQL query statement below:
Way 1: lead(num, 1) over (order by id): this retrives the num value from the next row
       lead(num, 2) over (order by id): this retrives the num value from two rows ahead
    by ordering the rows based on id
    => these two function help us compare the current row with the next two rows to check for repetition.
select distinct num as ConsecutiveNums
from (
    select num,
    lead(num, 1) over (order by id) as next_num,
    lead(num, 2) over (order by id) as next_2_num
    from Logs
) as consecutive_nums
where num = next_num and num = next_2_num

Way 2: using self-join => this is classic solution! joining the table to itself multiple times, offsetting the IDs to align consecutive rows.
select distinct l1.num as ConsecutiveNums
from Logs l1
join Logs l2 on l1.id = l2.id - 1
join Logs l3 on l2.id = l3.id - 1
where l1.num = l2.num and l2.num = l3.num